WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.280
[intro music]

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.220
Good morning, Hank. It's Friday,
February 9th, and it's laundry day.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.040
You will note that we have the
world's smallest washing machine,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.720
it's the only washing machine-slash-dryer that was
ever designed exclusively for Lilliputians.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.420
Let's see how many clothes it'll fit!

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:32.540
One shirt, one pair of boxers, one pair of pants,
two pairs of – wait what's this?

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.600
Oh, it's The Naked and the Dead in my pants.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.100
Two socks, one pair of boxers,
aaaand it's completely full.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.100
In fact, it's kind of overstuffed.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.300
I might have to take out one of the socks.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.600
Hank, I talked to Brotherhood 2.0 
resident mathematician Daniel Biss,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.460
and it turns out that there's a huge gaping flaw in your
mathematical theorem about old people and dying

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.460
and banjo concerts.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.260
The problem is this: a 70-year old may live
on average to be 83 years old,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.300
but that doesn't mean that a 71-year-old is
just as likely to die as an 82-year-old.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:10.060
In fact, the vast majority of people are going to die
closer to 82 than they are to 71.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.040
If you want to see a person die at a banjo concert,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.960
the key is not to go to banjo concerts
that feature older people,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.200
the key is to go to longer banjo concerts.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.080
Like, 13-year-long banjo concerts.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.840
Hank, several Brotherhood 2.0 commenters have
been asking three questions about my new book:

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.700
What's it called, when is it going to come out,
and what's it about?

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:38.540
I am now happy to answer all of those questions, although I'm going to answer one of them with a long series of lies.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.000
The book is called "Paper Towns", it will be
published in September of 2008,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.640
it's about life in an orphanage in Imperial
Japan in the 17th century.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.000
It's about the World Origami
Championship of 2004.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.980
It's the story of a special
boy and his special dog.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.360
I don't want to ruin it for you,
but the dog dies.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.740
It's about an ambiguously-gendered child
growing up in a Greek-American family.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:58.880
It's about Hamlet.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.500
It's a historical novel in which I imagine the 
teenage years of Emily Dickinson.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.980
It's about a face... that is half in the dark.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.660
And half in the light.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.580
It's about learning to come to terms with the fact that
even though sometimes you eat the exact number

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.500
of points you're supposed to eat,
you still don't get less fat.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:19.940
It's about how, when the last ding-dong of doom has
clanged and faded from the last rock hanging tideless

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.340
in the last red and dying evening, that even then there
will still be one more sound, that of man's puny,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.600
inexhaustible voice, still talking.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.380
It's about a girl named Becca who has
a very happy birthday.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.500
Hank, I'll be watching you on Monday from
lovely Washington, D.C.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:36.500
See you then!

